What to eat for breakfast on a low carb diet?
Although it is used massively to reduce weight, it is also a very efficient diet for controlling type 2 diabetes. Studies show that it is also effective in improving some autoimmune diseases, epilepsy, dyslipidemia, polycystic ovary syndrome and other cardiovascular diseases.
This term low carb refers to several strategies where you reduce carbohydrate in the diet, so that nutritious do not be the largest amount in the count of macronutrients in the diet. It is recommended to increase the amount of protein and fat to compensate for CHO depletion.
It is a strategy constituted by the reduction of the daily carbohydrate amount, and there may be several types of low carb diet, since there is no official guideline characterizing it. Thus, in general, it is characterized in the use of a maximum of 45% of carbohydrate from the VET (total energy value) or between 50 and 200 grams of CHO per day, and if there is less than 50 grams of CHO per day or 10% of the VET becomes a ketogenic diet.
And this causes a decrease in the stimulus to insulin, a hormone that acts by reducing lipolysis and increasing fat oxidation, causing stored fats to be used as a source of energy.
Insulin is responsible for controlling blood glucose. In a diet rich in carbohydrates, it is stimulated in excess, causing the body to develop a certain resistance, not being able to perform its function correctly, and consequently, the production of this hormone is reduced or even stopped.
With the decrease in carbohydrate intake, the body begins to use fat as the main source of energy, through fatty acids and ketones, initiating ketosis. This increases the oxidation of fats, increasing caloric expenditure.
Prioritized foods are protein and good fat sources, and low-carb fruits and vegetables.
– Full-fat dairy products: full-fat cheeses, full-fat yogurt and milk integral
