This machine is made of many components; it is primarily consisted of a hopper, a barrel containing a reciprocating screw, temperature control devices, a clamping unit and the injection mould itself.
The mould is created by the Toolmakers from the specifications given to them from the designer or engineer via a 3D computer-generated model.
This will allow easy ejection from the injection moulding machine when the part has cooled.
The specifications and components of the machine must be carefully set up before the process of injection moulding can begin.
Once the machine is set, the cycle will begin, and the machine can be left unattended to work its magic.
Raw material and colour is fed into the barrel of the machine via the hopper.
The reciprocating screw inside the barrel will turn, injecting a precise amount of the now molten plastic into the injection mould at a set and controlled temperature, pressure and speed.
When cool, the mould will open automatically.
If a portion of the finished part is still caught in the mould at this point the mould will reopen until the part has been completely ejected.
The autonomous nature of the injection moulding machine means it can be left unattended once programmed, and may run continuously throughout day and night.
