Situated in the north-west of Cochin-China, the province of Chaudoc is bounded on the north and on the west by the Kingdom of Cambodia, on the south, by the provinces of Hatien and Rachgia , and in the east, by the provinces of Longxuyen and Tanan .
This province, which has approximately an area of 275.876 hectares, is formed by an immense plain, with a towering range of seven mountains, of which the highest point is Nui Cam (880m), at a distance of 40km from the chief town. In the immediate vicinity of the chief town, is the Nui Sam , a much smaller mountain, 232 meters high, on the summit of which a sanatorium was built in 1896.
The climate of Chaudoc is fairly healthy, and the temperature varies between 18 and 26 degrees centigrade. It has a regular rainy season from May to October.
Administrative Geography The province of Chaudoc is divided into 12 cantons, formed into 4 administrative districts, at the head of which is placed a native administrative deleg ate.
