French Indochina or the Indochinese Union consists of five countries: Tonkin, Annam , Cochinchina, Cambodia, and Laos.
Cochinchina, bounded to the north by Cambodia and Annam, and to the east and west by the sea, is formed by the southern basin and delta of the Mekong River, a vast alluvial plain dominated on one side by the last foothills of Cambodia marking the Ha Tien Hill (Nui Sam, 215m) and the island of Phu Quoc , and on the other by the southern tip of the Annamite chain which ends at Nui Ba Den , or Tay Ninh Mountain (966m), to the mountain of Ba Ria (850m) and to the islets of Cape St. Jacques.
The main characteristic of the climate is that it is subject to the monsoon pattern, determining two very clear seasons: the rainy season from April to November and the dry season from December to March. Despite these monsoons, the climate is the same: the temperature ranges from 25 to 30 from one end of the year to the next.
